\name{Rserve.session.key}
\title{Session Key for Resuming Connections}
\alias{Rserve.session.key}
\usage{
Rserve.session.key()
}
\description{
  \code{Rserve.session.key} returns the key that a client can use to
  re-connect to the current session (i.e., the current forked Rserve
  process) after its connection was closed.

  The key is generated by the Rserve server process on the first call
  and stays the same for the life of the session. It is the only
  credential needed to resume the session, so it should only be given
  to the client that owns the session, over a secure channel.

  This function only works in a connection-serving process of a forked
  (unix) Rserve server with the configuration setting
  \code{session.resume enable}. It is not supported in servers started
  with \code{run.Rserve(background=TRUE)}.
}
\value{
  The session key as a string of 64 hexadecimal digits (32 bytes).
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
## inside an OCAP application
key <- Rserve.session.key()
}
}
\author{Simon Urbanek}
\keyword{interface}
